WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.661
[MUSIC]
Hi Shih.

00:00:01.661 --> 00:00:02.413
&gt;&gt; Hi.
[LAUGH]

00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:08.513
&gt;&gt; How does it feel-

00:00:08.513 --> 00:00:09.369
&gt;&gt; Hanging out for like 20 minutes.

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:12.441
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Still awkward, right?

00:00:12.441 --> 00:00:18.716
&gt;&gt; How does it feel to be back in front
of everyone you're not on Zoom anymore?

00:00:18.716 --> 00:00:20.363
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it feels nice.

00:00:20.363 --> 00:00:23.755
We had a game last night in Portland and

00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:29.064
it was first time really that
we felt a full house with fans.

00:00:29.064 --> 00:00:33.564
Last year at the end of the season we
had some fans back in the building but

00:00:33.564 --> 00:00:34.694
not a full house.

00:00:34.694 --> 00:00:39.087
And last night you could just feel
the energy was like everybody was so

00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:42.281
excited just to have some
semblance of normalcy.

00:00:42.281 --> 00:00:46.147
So this feels good to it's
good to be back with people.

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:50.855
&gt;&gt; Yeah hopefully we recreate that
energy or tonight going to stock.

00:00:50.855 --> 00:00:54.407
Before he got dive into the interview,
wanted to share,

00:00:54.407 --> 00:00:58.996
if you do want to show us the bottom
half of your face, you have two options.

00:00:58.996 --> 00:01:01.175
Stay hydrated so lots of water or

00:01:01.175 --> 00:01:06.181
option two we've been told as if this
was a performance in actually remove

00:01:06.181 --> 00:01:10.884
your mask if you want to shoot some
three pointers for us, let us know.

00:01:10.884 --> 00:01:14.028
&gt;&gt; So
I can actually remove this if I perform.

00:01:14.028 --> 00:01:14.918
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:01:14.918 --> 00:01:19.341
If you shoot your career percentage
tonight when you can automatically remove

00:01:19.341 --> 00:01:20.017
that yeah.

00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:26.685
Anyway, let's dive in, in all seriousness,
&gt;&gt; So the mask stays or goes which one?

00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:28.334
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't see a ball.

00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:29.365
I don't see who.

00:01:29.365 --> 00:01:31.757
&gt;&gt; Do we have a basketball
in the crowd here somewhere?

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:32.870
&gt;&gt; I could juggle maybe.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.016
&gt;&gt; A judge maybe any other
performance would also do?

00:01:36.016 --> 00:01:38.569
&gt;&gt; I think I really know
how to do anything else.

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:39.606
I should.

00:01:39.606 --> 00:01:41.029
&gt;&gt; You don't want you to put us to shame,
to be honest.

00:01:41.029 --> 00:01:45.522
I think that's the real reason
we don't have a basketball here.

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.630
But thank you for being here at the GSB.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.217
We're super excited.

00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:51.790
We think we have a lot to learn from you.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.418
Not just Steve Kerr, the player,
the general manager,

00:01:55.418 --> 00:01:59.890
the head coach, but also the podcast host,
the social commentator.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.845
So you want to dive in.

00:02:01.845 --> 00:02:03.440
Let's perhaps start with leadership.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.691
How would you describe your style
of leadership in your own words?

00:02:08.691 --> 00:02:15.203
&gt;&gt; I think I lead my team a little
bit like I raised my children.

00:02:15.203 --> 00:02:22.653
My wife and I did that together and
we always sort of gave them a lot of rope.

00:02:22.653 --> 00:02:25.545
But they kind of knew,

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:30.879
they were responsible for that freedom.

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:33.968
And so I believe in the same
kind of leadership with a team,

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:37.888
you can tell by the way we play,
we're pretty fast paced, we're risky.

00:02:37.888 --> 00:02:43.436
We shoot a lot of crazy shots,
especially Steph and Clay.

00:02:43.436 --> 00:02:48.094
But I believe that the freedom that they

00:02:48.094 --> 00:02:53.183
have makes them play more instinctively.

00:02:53.183 --> 00:02:57.484
And then ultimately,
when you're coaching a basketball team and

00:02:57.484 --> 00:02:59.644
the players, it's their team.

00:02:59.644 --> 00:03:02.689
And so you have to give them the team, but

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:06.911
give them the blueprint for
how it's all going to work.

00:03:06.911 --> 00:03:09.494
And once the culture is sad, and

00:03:09.494 --> 00:03:15.138
the idea is sad about what we're trying
to accomplish of let them go and

00:03:15.138 --> 00:03:20.608
then you just rein them in when you
feel like you need to rein them in.

00:03:20.608 --> 00:03:24.598
So I would say that's kind of my style.

00:03:24.598 --> 00:03:25.708
&gt;&gt; That's your style.

00:03:25.708 --> 00:03:27.216
I heard freedom, I heard rest.

00:03:27.216 --> 00:03:28.679
I also heard children.

00:03:28.679 --> 00:03:31.494
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I want to touch on that.

00:03:31.494 --> 00:03:33.175
Where does that come from?

00:03:33.175 --> 00:03:37.903
And I'm curious if this is from your own
time as a child and your upbringing.

00:03:37.903 --> 00:03:42.790
Where does that start you just described
to us where did you find that?

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.853
&gt;&gt; Probably my own parents.

00:03:44.853 --> 00:03:49.471
I was kind of a strange kid,
to be honest with you.

00:03:49.471 --> 00:03:53.271
I was super competitive
at a very young age,

00:03:53.271 --> 00:03:59.449
to the point where it was actually
kind of embarrassing for the family.

00:03:59.449 --> 00:04:03.062
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I say that only half jokingly but-

00:04:03.062 --> 00:04:03.704
&gt;&gt; Give us an example.

00:04:03.704 --> 00:04:06.471
What was the embarrassing part?

00:04:06.471 --> 00:04:08.725
&gt;&gt; So like when you have
a family Easter egg hunt.

00:04:08.725 --> 00:04:12.276
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I don't find the golden egg and

00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:19.364
throw a complete tantrum at the age of
five because I didn't find the golden egg.

00:04:19.364 --> 00:04:22.055
That's kind of embarrassing for
the parents.

00:04:22.055 --> 00:04:24.254
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] We call that
competitive at the GSB.

00:04:24.254 --> 00:04:29.508
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but that was kind of for
whatever reason,

00:04:29.508 --> 00:04:35.670
that was the way I was wired and
it carried over to sports.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.878
And so, I think my parents
were really interesting.

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:42.500
They gave me a lot of rope and

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.973
they somehow they knew when
they go to my baseball games.

00:04:47.973 --> 00:04:51.376
By the way, back then,
parents didn't go to every single game.

00:04:51.376 --> 00:04:56.906
They just like if it was a Thursday
there's no way mom and dad were going.

00:04:56.906 --> 00:05:02.354
It's like they might go on Saturday was
a little different back then I turn.

00:05:02.354 --> 00:05:06.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So sometimes I throw a complete fit.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:11.118
If I got out or in baseball or
missed a bunch of shots and whatever,

00:05:11.118 --> 00:05:14.040
I would just throw a complete tantrum,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.545
my parents didn't really say anything
until later, like a dinner time.

00:05:19.545 --> 00:05:20.356
They'd so

00:05:20.356 --> 00:05:26.396
they somehow they instinctively knew just
wait until I cooled off a little bit.

00:05:26.396 --> 00:05:31.560
And I think it was pretty powerful
because by that time you call off,

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.286
it's a lot easier to see what
you've actually done, right?

00:05:36.286 --> 00:05:39.472
So I had to learn how to kind
of tame that competitiveness and

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:41.268
channel in the right direction.

00:05:41.268 --> 00:05:43.011
And I think my parents
really helped me do that.

00:05:43.011 --> 00:05:47.116
And so
I think that probably is in some way,

00:05:47.116 --> 00:05:52.602
maybe subconsciously kind of
the way I guide my own team.

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:57.140
&gt;&gt; Right, I want to touch more on
your upbringing and your parents.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:59.345
You were born in Beirut.

00:05:59.345 --> 00:06:02.423
Your father,
Malcolm was also born in Beirut.

00:06:02.423 --> 00:06:05.884
There's a lot of movement
between Lebanon and Egypt,

00:06:05.884 --> 00:06:09.357
other Mediterranean countries and
then Los Angeles.

00:06:09.357 --> 00:06:11.857
Your father was a professor.

00:06:11.857 --> 00:06:17.271
Not many players or GMs or head coaches
have that experience at that age or

00:06:17.271 --> 00:06:19.415
that point of their career.

00:06:19.415 --> 00:06:24.122
How did that exposure to the international
culture impact you will have shaped

00:06:24.122 --> 00:06:28.128
not just the leadership style but
your worldview moving forward?

00:06:28.128 --> 00:06:33.028
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was probably that
the best education I received as

00:06:33.028 --> 00:06:36.361
a child growing up was living overseas.

00:06:36.361 --> 00:06:39.586
I was born in Beirut as you mentioned.

00:06:39.586 --> 00:06:44.731
I've lived in Cairo Egypt for about
three years when my dad was a visiting

00:06:44.731 --> 00:06:50.735
professor at the American University there
and we spent time in France and Tunisia.

00:06:50.735 --> 00:06:56.979
And so I had a really good experience in
terms of just seeing other cultures and

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:02.661
living in other cultures seeing
especially in Cairo abject poverty.

00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:09.561
And seeing how wonderful the people were
and yet how little so many of them had.

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:12.892
When you're 10, 12 years old,
that's really powerful.

00:07:12.892 --> 00:07:17.339
Especially coming from Los Angeles
where we were, you know,

00:07:17.339 --> 00:07:21.966
my dad was a professor at UCLA and
we spent a lot of time on campus.

00:07:21.966 --> 00:07:26.681
And it's kind of this idyllic,
seeing just like here in Stanford and

00:07:26.681 --> 00:07:30.423
some of the contrast, and
it's great perspective for

00:07:30.423 --> 00:07:33.289
a young person to feel
that at a young age.

00:07:33.289 --> 00:07:34.191
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:07:34.191 --> 00:07:39.952
&gt;&gt; And then I think you naturally sort
of build some empathy, but perspective.

00:07:39.952 --> 00:07:44.841
And I try to introduce perspective
with our team as often as I can

00:07:44.841 --> 00:07:49.649
at something Gregg Popovich was
amazing with in San Antonio.

00:07:49.649 --> 00:07:53.015
And I think it's really crucial just for

00:07:53.015 --> 00:07:58.164
all of our players to understand
how lucky we are to compete and

00:07:58.164 --> 00:08:01.840
play basketball for
a living and be able to,

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.358
Come into the gym every day and
be together, it's so much fun.

00:08:07.358 --> 00:08:12.613
And most of the world is trying to get by,
so we're really incredibly blessed.

00:08:12.613 --> 00:08:13.989
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:13.989 --> 00:08:17.737
&gt;&gt; Are there specific
stories from that childhood.

00:08:17.737 --> 00:08:21.073
The time in Cairo seeing the abject
poverty, that come to mind that

00:08:21.073 --> 00:08:24.424
I've started with you that you
perhaps have shared with players.

00:08:24.424 --> 00:08:28.900
Or colleagues or
your staff that you've held on to.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:33.352
&gt;&gt; There's an area in Cairo
called the City Of The Dead.

00:08:33.352 --> 00:08:39.149
And it's, when I was there, this was
in the early, late 70s, early 80s.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:46.349
There were like a million
people who lived basically in

00:08:46.349 --> 00:08:53.100
a cemetery underneath the seas,
the cemetery area.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:59.150
Obviously a pretty brutal nickname for
the place.

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:03.430
But I remember driving by it and asking,
why is it called the City Of The Dead?

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:08.740
Well, this is a burial ground and
a lot of people are,

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:15.040
living here because this is
the only space they have.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.227
How do you reconcile that with, life and

00:09:18.227 --> 00:09:22.371
Pacific Palisades going
to UCLA basketball games.

00:09:22.371 --> 00:09:26.355
And thinking,
the world was a pretty easy place to be.

00:09:26.355 --> 00:09:30.760
And so,
they got all really stuck with me forever.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks for sharing that.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.254
I feel like the way you
shared that in that emotion,

00:09:36.254 --> 00:09:40.040
it's still very live with you and
carry that on.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.950
You mentioned Gregg Popovich as well.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:46.373
One of, in my opinion at least personally,
I'm sure you'll agree.

00:09:46.373 --> 00:09:51.000
The best leaders out there and
not just coach but leader in the NBA.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.580
You've worked with him.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.466
You've also worked with
Phil Jackson at the Bulls.

00:09:55.466 --> 00:09:57.247
When your leadership style,

00:09:57.247 --> 00:10:01.246
is anything you found from either
of those two that you have drawn.

00:10:01.246 --> 00:10:04.960
And you would like to embody
yourself with your team.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.027
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,
almost everything that I do is

00:10:09.027 --> 00:10:13.530
a reflection of either Phil or
Pope or Lute Olson.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.308
But the thing that I really
learned as I went is that,

00:10:18.308 --> 00:10:23.250
if you're not authentic to yourself, your
players are going to feel that right away.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:26.840
So, while the influence is
there from all of those guys.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:30.213
And, definitely a lot of
the basketball drill work and

00:10:30.213 --> 00:10:33.379
the philosophy comes from the people,
I learned.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:36.426
Playing under,

00:10:36.426 --> 00:10:41.137
it still has to be my own values that
I share with the team every day.

00:10:41.137 --> 00:10:48.140
Because that's going to be authentic and
they'll feel that authenticity.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.755
I was really dealt an amazing
hand when I got here.

00:10:50.755 --> 00:10:59.000
Because, most first time coaches take over
bad team and we already had a good team.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:03.175
But, the opportunity to
coach Steph Curry and

00:11:03.175 --> 00:11:06.611
I could go on with a number of players.

00:11:06.611 --> 00:11:08.982
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; In this conversation but

00:11:08.982 --> 00:11:10.717
Steph in particular,

00:11:10.717 --> 00:11:16.760
because I think we share too really
crucial values that define our whole team.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:19.620
And that is joy and competitiveness.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.776
Steff is just vicious competitor, but

00:11:23.776 --> 00:11:29.848
you wouldn't know it because of the way
he looks and the joy that he plays with.

00:11:29.848 --> 00:11:33.062
We see the world in a similar way, we've,

00:11:33.062 --> 00:11:37.093
we understand the power of competing for
something.

00:11:37.093 --> 00:11:41.948
But the power of joy within that and
so finding that balance

00:11:41.948 --> 00:11:47.100
every day is kind of the key
to what makes our team tick.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:52.095
But to be able to coach someone
who shares your values like that,

00:11:52.095 --> 00:11:56.362
is just an incredible asset for
a coach, for a leader.

00:11:56.362 --> 00:12:03.420
And to have somebody like Steph who's so
strong in terms of his own leadership.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.885
It's been just incredibly blessed.

00:12:05.885 --> 00:12:08.457
&gt;&gt; Right, you touched on Steph, and

00:12:08.457 --> 00:12:12.850
I want to maybe dive deeper
into a part of leadership.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.510
And that's people management.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.598
A lot of us here at the GSB
will graduate entry jobs or

00:12:19.598 --> 00:12:22.889
we perhaps have to manage
our direct reports.

00:12:22.889 --> 00:12:26.767
And those beneath us in the org
chart as well as upwards,

00:12:26.767 --> 00:12:29.850
water managers or bosses and so on.

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:31.100
You've had a similar experience.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:35.890
You have to manage, GM,
President, operations, owners,

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:39.520
as well as your own staff,
players and so on.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:40.950
What framework do you use?

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:46.875
What's your mental model when thinking
about, leveraging authority, both upwards,

00:12:46.875 --> 00:12:52.483
downwards and sideways,
&gt;&gt; I think just being aware of everybody's

00:12:52.483 --> 00:12:58.174
challenges and trying to remind
yourself on a daily basis.

00:12:58.174 --> 00:13:04.302
That every single person in
the organization has challenges,

00:13:04.302 --> 00:13:08.940
vulnerabilities, strengths, weaknesses.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:15.207
We're all trying for the same thing, but
it's very natural for there to be blame.

00:13:15.207 --> 00:13:19.565
The front office might sign a player and
doesn't turn out,

00:13:19.565 --> 00:13:25.680
maybe they draft a player and they don't
think we're developing the player well.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.584
Or maybe our coaches are thinking well,

00:13:27.584 --> 00:13:30.110
we should have drafted
somebody like all that stuff.

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:33.448
There's the potential for
that every single day,

00:13:33.448 --> 00:13:35.780
just like in any business, right?

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.430
There's so many people,
when you get so many people involved.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:41.650
There's potential conflict.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.480
So, I just try to think of, obviously,

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.658
depending on what's
going on with the team.

00:13:47.658 --> 00:13:52.299
Just try to think each day of,
who do I need to check in with and

00:13:52.299 --> 00:13:55.497
maybe it's just a phone call or an email.

00:13:55.497 --> 00:14:00.602
But maintaining those lines of
communication constantly like you said,

00:14:00.602 --> 00:14:03.170
managing in every direction.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:05.440
It's really crucial.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:05.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.120
&gt;&gt; And it's a big part of the job, right?

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:09.772
&gt;&gt; You mentioned communication there and

00:14:09.772 --> 00:14:11.679
I want to maybe touch on
that a little bit more.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:17.625
This is specifically around creating
a culture that is both diverse and

00:14:17.625 --> 00:14:18.730
inclusive.

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:20.110
So, we hear that a lot.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:24.363
I want to focus more on the inclusive part
which is not just having a diverse set

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:25.969
of individuals on the team.

00:14:25.969 --> 00:14:28.050
But making them feel like they belong.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.318
They have a voice,
there is open communication,

00:14:30.318 --> 00:14:32.429
regardless of where they
are in the org chart.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:37.829
I think back it was in June 2015,
NBA Finals Game 4,

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:41.480
you started Andre Iguodala for
the first time.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.400
And I believe there was
a Wall Street Journal article about this.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:49.560
That a 28 year old video assistant
who was fairly junior on your staff,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:53.270
came up with an analysis
do from Q to do that.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:56.579
Can you tell us more about kind
of what that story wasn't there,

00:14:56.579 --> 00:15:00.000
was a 3AM Jack's message also
involved in there somewhere.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.972
What enabled a junior staff member to
have an impact which then automate on to

00:15:04.972 --> 00:15:06.070
the Finals MVP?

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:10.675
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, it's one of the things
I really learned from Pope,

00:15:10.675 --> 00:15:12.451
playing in San Antonio.

00:15:12.451 --> 00:15:16.680
He used to say all the time,
anybody can have a good idea.

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:18.820
It doesn't matter where it comes from.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:23.050
Let's explore everything and if one of
you has a thought, bring it up, and

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:25.685
he would say the same
thing to all the coaches.

00:15:25.685 --> 00:15:30.050
And so it was very inclusive there and
I thought that was really powerful.

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:34.859
And I wanted to create
the same sort of culture here.

00:15:34.859 --> 00:15:39.706
And so that was the way we operated,
we sort of embraced all ideas.

00:15:39.706 --> 00:15:43.808
I encouraged everybody, all the coaches
to talk during during meetings.

00:15:43.808 --> 00:15:46.693
And if it had something to say
to try to get it out of them.

00:15:46.693 --> 00:15:49.950
And same thing in the huddles,
take a timeout and yeah,

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:55.040
we might see something as a coaching staff
and the players might feel differently.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.160
And, so, I'll sometimes,
I'll say in the timeout, hey,

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:01.786
I'm thinking we should do this,
what are you guys think.

00:16:01.786 --> 00:16:04.689
And usually it's Draymond who says,
no, we should do something different.

00:16:04.689 --> 00:16:07.828
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But Draymond's really smart, and

00:16:07.828 --> 00:16:08.957
he's the one who is out there.

00:16:08.957 --> 00:16:13.540
And so a lot of times, like, all right,
if you guys feel strongly about that,

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:14.522
let's do that.

00:16:14.522 --> 00:16:17.221
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:17.221 --> 00:16:22.051
&gt;&gt; But to do that, you have to be
comfortable in your own skin, and

00:16:22.051 --> 00:16:27.539
you have to embrace, I think,
a really crucial aspect of leadership.

00:16:27.539 --> 00:16:32.984
Which is that you actually will
come across stronger to the people

00:16:32.984 --> 00:16:39.130
you're leading if you show that you
don't have all the answers, right?

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:43.572
I think sometimes leaders feel like,
my gosh, I've gotta have all the answers,

00:16:43.572 --> 00:16:46.090
otherwise, they're not
going to respect me.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:50.371
I think it's sort of the opposite, as long
as they know you're putting in the work

00:16:50.371 --> 00:16:52.807
and that you know what
you're talking about.

00:16:52.807 --> 00:16:57.366
Because what we're doing is more art
than science, it's a basketball game.

00:16:57.366 --> 00:17:01.390
So there's no perfect answer for anything.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:07.897
So if you can be collaborative and
sometimes allow the players to make

00:17:07.897 --> 00:17:13.045
a decision or the video coordinator-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:13.045 --> 00:17:15.750
&gt;&gt; There shouldn't be any hesitation to do

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:20.050
that as long as you don't blame
that person if it doesn't work.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:23.579
You gotta own the decision afterwards,

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:28.261
and whatever it is, and
give credit if credit is due.

00:17:28.261 --> 00:17:34.133
But if you just do that and it comes
naturally and you feel comfortable with

00:17:34.133 --> 00:17:40.492
that, it's a really powerful force
because then the players take ownership.

00:17:40.492 --> 00:17:47.351
But doing that while maintaining your,
Your sense of you're

00:17:47.351 --> 00:17:52.711
the person in charge ultimately is kind of
the balance that we're all looking for.

00:17:52.711 --> 00:17:55.933
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, I see a lot of
similarities with what you said earlier,

00:17:55.933 --> 00:17:58.505
about leadership is giving that freedom,
that rope.

00:17:58.505 --> 00:18:02.212
Allowing them to feel like they're part of
the process and they have ownership and

00:18:02.212 --> 00:18:05.390
then actually being the decision
maker with all the inputs yourself.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:12.185
Perhaps, more specifically
on people management,

00:18:12.185 --> 00:18:17.012
there was a situation I
believe back in November 2018.

00:18:17.012 --> 00:18:21.639
The Warriors were playing the Clippers
down in LA, nationally televised game.

00:18:21.639 --> 00:18:25.787
There was a confrontation on television
between Kevin Durant and Draymond Green,

00:18:25.787 --> 00:18:27.620
two of your superstars.

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:31.974
When you had to deal with that situation,
that confrontation, what learnings from

00:18:31.974 --> 00:18:36.045
your past experiences, you mentioned
Popovich a couple of times and others.

00:18:36.045 --> 00:18:39.840
What did you lean on in that moment
to diffuse that situation and

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:41.629
move on from that situation,

00:18:41.629 --> 00:18:47.070
which was potentially a pretty difficult
confrontation that had just occurred?

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:52.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that one was a tough one, I had
never experienced anything like that,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.128
where two players got into
it in a game like that.

00:18:55.128 --> 00:18:58.070
So I think that's more
instinct than anything.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:04.458
I think as a leader, as a coach,
you have to have kind of non-negotiables.

00:19:04.458 --> 00:19:09.550
The players need to know
what line can be crossed or

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:16.353
what line can't be crossed, and
just felt like that was too much.

00:19:16.353 --> 00:19:21.828
And Bob Myers and I got together and
handled it the way we thought was best.

00:19:21.828 --> 00:19:27.947
There was no easy answer, and we just
tried to do the best we could with it,

00:19:27.947 --> 00:19:30.830
but it was very, very difficult.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:35.916
And obviously, when you're doing it
with the public staring at you and

00:19:35.916 --> 00:19:38.715
knowing everything that's at stake,

00:19:38.715 --> 00:19:43.060
it's not an easy position to be in,
but it's going to happen.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.889
When you're in a leadership position,

00:19:44.889 --> 00:19:46.942
there are very difficult
decisions to make.

00:19:46.942 --> 00:19:50.769
And you just kind of have to do what you
think is best, I think, and what's right.

00:19:50.769 --> 00:19:54.049
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, and like you said,
in your career over many decades,

00:19:54.049 --> 00:19:56.652
that was the first time you
had even come across that.

00:19:56.652 --> 00:20:00.444
So there's always a curveball around the
corner, there's always a first time for

00:20:00.444 --> 00:20:01.210
any situation.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.337
I'm sure you've learned a lot from that.

00:20:04.337 --> 00:20:08.421
What have you taken away and kind of
what's stuck with you going forward?

00:20:08.421 --> 00:20:13.728
Now it's almost three years on,
how has that incident and dealing with

00:20:13.728 --> 00:20:19.900
that new scenario changed your opinion or
style in people management?

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:23.629
&gt;&gt; Well, how many people here are going to
tweet about it if I answer it with-

00:20:23.629 --> 00:20:25.302
&gt;&gt; Yeah, keep your hands down, yeah.

00:20:25.302 --> 00:20:32.463
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] This is part of modern life,
it's really difficult.

00:20:32.463 --> 00:20:37.961
It's harder and
harder to live a public life and

00:20:37.961 --> 00:20:43.195
really open up and
share things that happen.

00:20:43.195 --> 00:20:47.709
Because all of a sudden,
things come right back around.

00:20:47.709 --> 00:20:51.841
And so that's one that was very,
very difficult to deal with.

00:20:51.841 --> 00:20:55.210
And obviously, Draymond brought
it up a couple months back, and

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:56.873
then it started all over again.

00:20:56.873 --> 00:21:00.883
And so not my favorite-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:00.883 --> 00:21:01.567
&gt;&gt; Topic to

00:21:01.567 --> 00:21:02.839
discuss, so-

00:21:02.839 --> 00:21:03.627
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:21:03.627 --> 00:21:05.043
&gt;&gt; Can we talk about
the championships that-

00:21:05.043 --> 00:21:05.694
&gt;&gt; Great, yeah.

00:21:05.694 --> 00:21:07.563
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's talk about that.

00:21:07.563 --> 00:21:09.816
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:09.816 --> 00:21:14.300
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:15.277
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:15.277 --> 00:21:17.459
&gt;&gt; And hopefully another one coming up

00:21:17.459 --> 00:21:20.285
next year in terms of championships-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:21:20.285 --> 00:21:23.721
&gt;&gt; So yeah.
&gt;&gt; Good, yeah.

00:21:23.721 --> 00:21:27.934
&gt;&gt; I want to dive into perhaps
decision making, Steve.

00:21:27.934 --> 00:21:31.434
And perhaps I want to go back to
a couple of different scenarios, and

00:21:31.434 --> 00:21:33.998
if you could share with us
your thought process or

00:21:33.998 --> 00:21:38.950
how you came to those conclusions,
decisions, I think that would be helpful.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:42.377
The first one was fairly early on
in your time with the Warriors.

00:21:42.377 --> 00:21:46.363
I believe there was a potential trade for
Kevin Love,

00:21:46.363 --> 00:21:50.880
who was just coming off a stellar year,
with Klay Thompson,

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:55.690
who at the time was still
fairly young in his NBA career.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:58.679
I believe you were very much for
Klay staying on the team and

00:21:58.679 --> 00:21:59.907
not having that trade.

00:21:59.907 --> 00:22:03.355
And of course, as they say, the rest
is history, Klay's been an all star and

00:22:03.355 --> 00:22:04.830
won three titles.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:10.817
What gave you conviction in that moment,
backing and supporting a fairly unknown or

00:22:10.817 --> 00:22:15.390
unproven player versus more of
a genuine kind of proven expert?

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:20.936
&gt;&gt; I think that I've been in the NBA now,
in some capacity, for 33 years.

00:22:20.936 --> 00:22:24.997
And one of the things I've learned
is the only thing you can be

00:22:24.997 --> 00:22:28.509
sure of is what's already
been proven on the court.

00:22:28.509 --> 00:22:34.140
Any time you decide to draft someone or
trade for someone, if there's no history,

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:39.600
if there's no proof that it's going to
work, you're really taking a chance.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.476
And so when I became the Warriors coach,

00:22:42.476 --> 00:22:48.399
the undisputed truth was that the Warriors
had the fourth best defense in the league

00:22:48.399 --> 00:22:53.570
with Klay Thompson guarding a lot of
the point guards that are out there.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:58.377
And it had become a really pick-and-roll
dominant game by that point.

00:22:58.377 --> 00:23:02.516
Now it's even more so, and
you've got Klay and Steph.

00:23:02.516 --> 00:23:06.642
And from a basketball standpoint, you'd
like to be able to protect Steph because

00:23:06.642 --> 00:23:10.845
he's going to have to handle the ball so
much, maybe put Klay on the point guard.

00:23:10.845 --> 00:23:14.295
I mean, and it had already worked, and so

00:23:14.295 --> 00:23:19.926
our thinking was we know the Warriors
have a great defense already.

00:23:19.926 --> 00:23:23.388
So if we can improve the offense
a little bit, and we thought we could.

00:23:23.388 --> 00:23:29.325
Watching the tape, we thought a lot of
young guys who, with some more seasoning,

00:23:29.325 --> 00:23:33.558
could generate better shots,
and it was all right there.

00:23:33.558 --> 00:23:39.590
Whereas you make a big deal, even if it's
for a great player, you just don't know.

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:45.774
And so I think, in most cases, you're
wise to stick with what you do know,

00:23:45.774 --> 00:23:50.209
if what you do know is really positive,
which it was.

00:23:50.209 --> 00:23:54.829
&gt;&gt; Right, right, and where did
the training of that eye and that talent,

00:23:54.829 --> 00:23:56.678
that conviction come from?

00:23:56.678 --> 00:23:59.181
Was it just seeing Klay and
Steph in the prior seasons and

00:23:59.181 --> 00:24:01.370
the shootarounds in the trainings?

00:24:01.370 --> 00:24:05.111
Or was there something else in your
experiences that made you believe that,

00:24:05.111 --> 00:24:08.152
no, this can come true,
let's just give it some more time and

00:24:08.152 --> 00:24:09.621
actually see this come true?

00:24:09.621 --> 00:24:14.157
&gt;&gt; Yeah these guys were
pretty young at the time, and

00:24:14.157 --> 00:24:17.290
were already really proficient.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:20.946
Steph was already an all star,
Clay hadn't been one yet.

00:24:20.946 --> 00:24:24.892
But you could see the potential, and
they were in the playoffs for two years.

00:24:24.892 --> 00:24:28.924
I mean, they were great,
this is a great team.

00:24:28.924 --> 00:24:33.267
I inherited a team that was
the 150 games that have been

00:24:33.267 --> 00:24:36.445
the playoffs the previous couple years.

00:24:36.445 --> 00:24:39.961
The previous staff under
Mark Jackson had done a great job.

00:24:39.961 --> 00:24:45.320
Establishing a defense, Warriors hadn't
had a defense in literally like 30 years.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:47.929
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; So they were the team that just scored

00:24:47.929 --> 00:24:51.839
125 points, and
usually the other team scored 127, and

00:24:51.839 --> 00:24:56.667
that was kind of, they were fun to watch,
but they weren't going anywhere.

00:24:56.667 --> 00:24:58.945
And so it was established, under Mark,

00:24:58.945 --> 00:25:02.094
that they were going to become
a great defensive team, and

00:25:02.094 --> 00:25:05.914
the front office did a really good
job putting the pieces together.

00:25:05.914 --> 00:25:10.890
So when we got here,
when my staff got here, it was like,

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:14.738
this is real,
we could win the whole thing.

00:25:14.738 --> 00:25:20.044
And that's why ultimately we
decided to stay the course,

00:25:20.044 --> 00:25:23.619
and it's always Bob's decision, and

00:25:23.619 --> 00:25:28.615
Bob was right with me in terms
of seeing where we can go.

00:25:28.615 --> 00:25:31.627
Because we already had a really
good start with the momentum they

00:25:31.627 --> 00:25:34.830
had built the previous couple of years,
right?

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:37.770
&gt;&gt; You mentioned inheriting a good team,
and

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:41.616
it was your first season as
a head coach at the NBA level.

00:25:41.616 --> 00:25:45.343
And you win their first title in 40 years,
I believe,

00:25:45.343 --> 00:25:48.605
the next year you win 73
regular season games.

00:25:48.605 --> 00:25:52.430
You break your own team, 72,
then record of the Bulls.

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:57.024
A lot of people here also
potentially will experience fairly

00:25:57.024 --> 00:26:01.087
rapid rises to success,
almost overnight success.

00:26:01.087 --> 00:26:06.481
What, if anything, changes, Steve,
in your approach to management,

00:26:06.481 --> 00:26:11.092
and just kind of how you approach
your day-to-day at the job,

00:26:11.092 --> 00:26:16.312
when you've now gone from a challenger
to now at the top of that throne,

00:26:16.312 --> 00:26:18.770
and to being that champion?

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:20.631
&gt;&gt; Really, nothing changed.

00:26:20.631 --> 00:26:26.053
It was, I think,
the routine of making sure we're staying

00:26:26.053 --> 00:26:31.042
locked in on the goals that we had,
trying to improve on

00:26:31.042 --> 00:26:36.800
the certain keys that we thought
would take us to the next step.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:43.224
And so, I think the whole idea with
anything is to build a culture,

00:26:43.224 --> 00:26:47.815
kind of an atmosphere that
the players are going to

00:26:47.815 --> 00:26:53.520
feel whether you're winning or
you're losing.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.767
And the whole point is to help
the players get better, and

00:26:56.767 --> 00:27:00.620
help them have the best
possible careers they can have.

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.688
And so that's true whether
you're winning championships or

00:27:03.688 --> 00:27:07.900
you have the worst record in the league,
the goal still is the same thing, right?

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:12.324
So, as long as you're generating
that feeling in the gym each day,

00:27:12.324 --> 00:27:15.584
then you have to be
comfortable with the criticism

00:27:15.584 --> 00:27:19.810
that's going to come your
way when you're losing.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:24.810
Because that's all part of it, and
it's just part of the territory.

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:31.229
&gt;&gt; Right, and how do you think about
managing, perhaps, the pressure and

00:27:31.229 --> 00:27:37.400
kind of the magnifying glass that
comes with being at that job?

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:39.750
And you said the criticism, the blame.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:43.650
You mentioned nothing changes day-to-day,
but

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:48.729
as we draw from students into
the post-graduation, real world,

00:27:48.729 --> 00:27:53.534
how do we think about using
overnight success more tangibly and

00:27:53.534 --> 00:27:59.246
thinking about our next steps in a way
that, perhaps, you were previously

00:27:59.246 --> 00:28:04.709
not exposed to, and you were not at
the top, and not in that limelight?

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:07.900
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:13.110
I think life is more difficult now
than it's ever been for all of us.

00:28:13.110 --> 00:28:15.550
Whether you're in
a position of leadership or

00:28:15.550 --> 00:28:19.460
if you're a young person trying to
figure out what you're going to do.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:24.243
Just the nature of life now
with the constant criticism and

00:28:24.243 --> 00:28:27.960
judgment that we all
have at our fingertips.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:30.930
That's so difficult to avoid.

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:36.215
I know when I played, literally,
if you wanted to avoid any of the chatter,

00:28:36.215 --> 00:28:39.281
you just didn't pick up the morning paper.

00:28:39.281 --> 00:28:44.143
And I had an experience in Chicago,
I was trying out for the Bulls, and

00:28:44.143 --> 00:28:46.863
I had a non-guaranteed contract, and

00:28:46.863 --> 00:28:50.997
I was just trying to make the team,
and I was playing great.

00:28:50.997 --> 00:28:53.948
And I decided to pick up
the paper one day, thinking,

00:28:53.948 --> 00:28:56.777
maybe they're going to say
something nice about me.

00:28:56.777 --> 00:28:59.147
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Pick it up, and

00:28:59.147 --> 00:29:02.399
you can probably guess
where the story is going.

00:29:02.399 --> 00:29:06.074
The writer says, well, the Bulls are
going to make cuts in the next couple of

00:29:06.074 --> 00:29:09.158
days, and Steve Kerr's probably
going to get cut, and so and so

00:29:09.158 --> 00:29:10.550
is probably going to get cut.

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:14.792
And I was crushed, I was like, what is
he talking about, I'm playing so well.

00:29:14.792 --> 00:29:17.107
The next day at practice, I was terrible.

00:29:17.107 --> 00:29:20.719
And so I think about that
story sometimes because,

00:29:20.719 --> 00:29:24.673
now it's like times a thousand for
everybody, right?

00:29:24.673 --> 00:29:29.660
Whether you're trying out for an NBA team
or just trying to get through high school.

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:36.005
Hearing all the judgment and
the criticism, and it's brutal.

00:29:36.005 --> 00:29:38.380
So, how do you navigate that?

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:40.460
How do you avoid that?

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:46.187
And so, finding balance, what I've found
with our players is the guys who have

00:29:46.187 --> 00:29:51.758
the most balance in their life are the
ones who handle all this stuff the best.

00:29:51.758 --> 00:29:55.216
Work-life balance is just crucial.

00:29:55.216 --> 00:29:59.789
The days of coaches sleeping
in their office all night,

00:29:59.789 --> 00:30:03.877
maybe there are still
coaches out there doing it,

00:30:03.877 --> 00:30:08.075
probably in the NFL,
I think that's their thing.

00:30:08.075 --> 00:30:14.964
But it's just insane, I think you have to
have a life, you have to have balance.

00:30:14.964 --> 00:30:18.584
And Steph has a great example,
he loves life.

00:30:18.584 --> 00:30:22.088
He loves his family,
he loves playing golf,

00:30:22.088 --> 00:30:26.710
he loves being with his kids,
and being at home for dinner.

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:31.290
And every day is a gift to him,
and so he takes plenty of heat.

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:33.171
He takes plenty of criticism.

00:30:33.171 --> 00:30:38.255
But he's able to manage it and navigate
it, and we've had a lot of players

00:30:38.255 --> 00:30:42.938
who haven't been able to because
their lives aren't as balanced.

00:30:42.938 --> 00:30:49.295
And so I guess that's my advice,
is to really seek balance,

00:30:49.295 --> 00:30:53.672
that work-life relationship balance.

00:30:53.672 --> 00:30:58.734
And I find it through,
I do a lot of yoga, I like to read.

00:30:58.734 --> 00:31:00.938
I find if I can get into a good book,

00:31:00.938 --> 00:31:04.223
I'm just kind of in a better
frame of mind each day.

00:31:04.223 --> 00:31:10.411
And I like to cook,
just cooking a meal at home.

00:31:10.411 --> 00:31:13.340
I'm not a chef, but
if I can find a good recipe,

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:15.768
I just go buy the ingredients and cook.

00:31:15.768 --> 00:31:19.762
And have a game on in the background,
and have a beer, that's a great day.

00:31:19.762 --> 00:31:21.276
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that sounds fun.

00:31:21.276 --> 00:31:24.111
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but
that helps me when people say,

00:31:24.111 --> 00:31:28.164
why the hell doesn't Kerr run
more pick and roll for Curry?

00:31:28.164 --> 00:31:31.483
I can just cook a meal and have a beer.

00:31:31.483 --> 00:31:35.892
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that must be nice.

00:31:35.892 --> 00:31:37.948
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, like you said,

00:31:37.948 --> 00:31:42.100
not all your players have that balance,
you mentioned Steph, and he does that.

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:49.270
What is your role as a head coach to,
is it a nudge in the right direction?

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:53.220
Is it more explicit than that?

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:57.967
How do you want to kind of corral as
many of your roster towards that?

00:31:57.967 --> 00:31:59.964
because you've clearly seen
the benefits of that balance.

00:31:59.964 --> 00:32:01.726
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:32:01.726 --> 00:32:02.814
&gt;&gt; How do you do that?

00:32:02.814 --> 00:32:03.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:32:03.980 --> 00:32:07.550
I think that's one area
where perspective comes in,

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:12.930
reminding Players,
just how fortunate we are.

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:18.570
And you can do that in variety of
ways bringing in guest speakers.

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:23.646
We're very lucky because people
want to come speak to the warriors,

00:32:23.646 --> 00:32:29.320
and so we're able to get a lot of
really interesting people to come in.

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:36.544
And we've had Michael Lewis came in,
the author came in to speak to our team.

00:32:36.544 --> 00:32:40.608
We had Dr. Dre and
Jimmy Iovine came in and

00:32:40.608 --> 00:32:45.844
did give a presentation on their company,
on Beats.

00:32:45.844 --> 00:32:49.803
We've had Alicia Keys and she came in and

00:32:49.803 --> 00:32:55.876
talked about her process of
writing a song and writing music.

00:32:55.876 --> 00:33:00.975
We've had Tommy Smith,
the sprinter from the 1968 Olympics,

00:33:00.975 --> 00:33:04.110
who held up his fist for racial equality.

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:08.897
Some incredible guests and so
trying to present something

00:33:08.897 --> 00:33:13.860
to the players that is not just,
here's how we're guarding, pick and roll.

00:33:13.860 --> 00:33:15.794
It's like this is-
&gt;&gt; This is life.

00:33:15.794 --> 00:33:16.751
&gt;&gt; This is life.

00:33:16.751 --> 00:33:23.155
Right, and then beyond that, we try to
offer them as much assistance as we can in

00:33:23.155 --> 00:33:28.528
terms of we have a mindfulness
coach to help them with meditation.

00:33:28.528 --> 00:33:33.959
We'll do some of that as a team and
then they'll have private

00:33:33.959 --> 00:33:39.710
sessions where we try to help
people to get comfortable learning

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:44.839
how to perform under all this
judgment we all live under.

00:33:44.839 --> 00:33:50.493
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah, and
perhaps this is judgment.

00:33:50.493 --> 00:33:55.460
Sometimes, I see a double standard for
the athletes and the people under

00:33:55.460 --> 00:34:01.160
that spotlight versus the rest of us
who are in a more of an office 95 job.

00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:04.583
For example, a lot of jet
companies during the Bay Area,

00:34:04.583 --> 00:34:07.070
have unlimited paid time off.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:09.590
They have unlimited vacation days and
so on.

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:14.271
When that say LeBron James does it,
it's called load management.

00:34:14.271 --> 00:34:18.937
If he misses the Primetime game, people
are upset, their television rights and

00:34:18.937 --> 00:34:20.930
sponsors and so on.

00:34:20.930 --> 00:34:21.653
Is that fair?

00:34:21.653 --> 00:34:25.886
Is that a double standard that we have
as a society between our athletes and

00:34:25.886 --> 00:34:27.040
everyone else?

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:29.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but that's part of the deal.

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:33.611
We try to talk to our team every
year about what the NBA is about.

00:34:33.611 --> 00:34:37.049
When you're playing in the NBA,
you're going to get booed.

00:34:37.049 --> 00:34:39.933
You're going to get criticized.

00:34:39.933 --> 00:34:40.824
You're going to get injured.

00:34:40.824 --> 00:34:43.065
You're going to get cut,
you're going to get traded.

00:34:43.065 --> 00:34:48.053
But if you can navigate it well, it's an
amazing job and you're going to make a lot

00:34:48.053 --> 00:34:51.851
of money and you're going to
have some great experiences, and

00:34:51.851 --> 00:34:55.892
you get to play basketball for
a living and this is the trade off.

00:34:55.892 --> 00:34:58.250
So you got to understand that right away.

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:02.479
I once had, I won't name,
this teammate, but

00:35:02.479 --> 00:35:07.410
when I was late in my career,
I was playing for the Blazers.

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:11.942
And one of our rookies on that
team would sit with me and

00:35:11.942 --> 00:35:16.085
another veteran player
guy named Chris Dudley.

00:35:16.085 --> 00:35:18.683
And the coach,
Maurice Cheeks was the coach,

00:35:18.683 --> 00:35:22.678
I think he wanted this rookie to sit
with us so we could share our wisdom,

00:35:22.678 --> 00:35:26.891
we were both getting ready to retire,
we'd been in the league a long time.

00:35:26.891 --> 00:35:33.220
And this player, first week of the season,
he says he's exhausted.

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:35.877
It's been a month of training camp and
games and

00:35:35.877 --> 00:35:38.013
we're maybe a month into the season.

00:35:38.013 --> 00:35:42.614
He says, hey guys, I have a question, and
he says, how much time do we get off for

00:35:42.614 --> 00:35:43.376
Christmas?

00:35:43.376 --> 00:35:45.832
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Chris and

00:35:45.832 --> 00:35:50.611
I kind of looked at each other like,
boy, this is going to be a long year.

00:35:50.611 --> 00:35:51.520
&gt;&gt; He's got a lot to learn.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:56.438
&gt;&gt; We don't have any time off for
Christmas, you can see he was heartbroken,

00:35:56.438 --> 00:35:59.940
he said, we don't get any time off at all?

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:03.544
And no, and it turned out the coach
actually made him go home for

00:36:03.544 --> 00:36:05.726
a couple of days, he was so homesick.

00:36:05.726 --> 00:36:06.949
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:36:06.949 --> 00:36:10.530
you kind of have to know
what you're getting into.

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:15.479
And one of the problems right now
with the league, it's not a problem,

00:36:15.479 --> 00:36:21.590
it's more of an issue that players are
coming in at a younger and younger age.

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:26.108
We just drafted two guys
in the lottery who are 19,

00:36:26.108 --> 00:36:31.361
they each spent one year after
high school and here they are.

00:36:31.361 --> 00:36:35.715
25 years ago, It was you know guys
like Tim Duncan and Patrick Ewing.

00:36:35.715 --> 00:36:42.209
We're playing four years in college and
doing what you do in college, growing up.

00:36:42.209 --> 00:36:46.076
So to have to grow up in the NBA at 19 and

00:36:46.076 --> 00:36:50.884
not really knowing anything
is very difficult,

00:36:50.884 --> 00:36:54.414
so we got to try to do the best we can.

00:36:54.414 --> 00:36:59.334
&gt;&gt; Right, I want to move on to perhaps
some of the work you did last year while

00:36:59.334 --> 00:37:04.090
the world was in lockdown, on you had a,
if I trust all flying coach,

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:06.150
I believe with Pete Carroll.

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:11.136
And there was a specific episode,
I believe that was June, early June,

00:37:11.136 --> 00:37:14.078
when you had Brad Popovich as it asked,
and

00:37:14.078 --> 00:37:17.371
this was right after
the George Floyd murders.

00:37:17.371 --> 00:37:22.196
And you described your
thoughts on that issue.

00:37:22.196 --> 00:37:26.597
You described how a player of yours,
I believe Andre Iguodala,

00:37:26.597 --> 00:37:31.487
had to tell you about the Tulsa riots,
despite you having been a student

00:37:31.487 --> 00:37:35.174
of American history in both
high school and college.

00:37:35.174 --> 00:37:40.054
And I think the words used to describe the
moment was an absence of leadership in our

00:37:40.054 --> 00:37:41.820
nation as a disease.

00:37:41.820 --> 00:37:46.630
This is very much issue with
specifically the white population.

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:52.610
About a year and a half on, Steve, what
are your thoughts on those topics today?

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:53.582
How have we changed?

00:37:53.582 --> 00:37:55.630
How have we improved, if at all?

00:37:55.630 --> 00:38:00.183
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's so hard because we're being
held back by the incredible division that

00:38:00.183 --> 00:38:02.797
exists, the political
division that exists.

00:38:02.797 --> 00:38:10.099
What Andre asked me, he said,
did you ever hear of the Tulsa race riot?

00:38:10.099 --> 00:38:13.508
I said no, this was year and a half ago.

00:38:13.508 --> 00:38:18.586
Since that time, a lot has come out,
there's been documentaries.

00:38:18.586 --> 00:38:24.351
But my point of the time was, I took
several years of American history and

00:38:24.351 --> 00:38:27.010
never heard one word about that.

00:38:27.010 --> 00:38:32.607
Our curriculum in high school and
college when it came to African American

00:38:32.607 --> 00:38:37.393
history was mainly Jackie Robinson,
and Martin Luther King,

00:38:37.393 --> 00:38:42.220
and these figures who we celebrated for
good reason.

00:38:42.220 --> 00:38:44.060
But we didn't flip it around.

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:45.669
We never said, okay,

00:38:45.669 --> 00:38:51.028
why was it that Jackie Robinson wasn't
allowed to play in the big leagues?

00:38:51.028 --> 00:38:54.329
Why weren't black people allowed to
play in the big leagues before that?

00:38:54.329 --> 00:38:57.055
We didn't ever really explore
that in school, right?

00:38:57.055 --> 00:39:01.981
And so
now there's this movement a year ago and

00:39:01.981 --> 00:39:07.664
I think everybody who's
involved in this movement,

00:39:07.664 --> 00:39:14.495
the whole point is we need to
reconcile some of our sins, right?

00:39:14.495 --> 00:39:16.005
Our original sin especially.

00:39:16.005 --> 00:39:17.640
We need to exempt.

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:21.350
We can't just ignore it,
which is what we've done.

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:26.240
Because you see how it's
manifested itself in so many ways.

00:39:26.240 --> 00:39:31.718
George Floyd's murder being an easy
example and a terrible example.

00:39:31.718 --> 00:39:35.373
But I think the point is,
what's happened now is so

00:39:35.373 --> 00:39:40.048
you immediately get this conversation
on critical race theory,

00:39:40.048 --> 00:39:43.873
which is just nothing more
than a fancy way of saying,

00:39:43.873 --> 00:39:48.210
hey let's teach real African American
history to our kids.

00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:52.206
And it seems so simple, right?

00:39:52.206 --> 00:39:58.194
It's like, yeah, that would be really
good, and maybe kids could grow up and

00:39:58.194 --> 00:40:04.030
learn that racism is really bad, and
teach them at a very young age, right?

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:07.980
Because it's clearly something
that is sort of taught.

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:11.089
And yet all of a sudden that
become the argument is, well,

00:40:11.089 --> 00:40:15.890
we don't want to teach our kids that
they're bad and they're bad people like.

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:17.510
Nobody's suggesting that.

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:22.330
But this is how every issue that we
have in this country now becomes.

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:28.358
It's like this and it's all for political
gain and it's really demoralizing.

00:40:28.358 --> 00:40:33.863
And it's just another example of how we're

00:40:33.863 --> 00:40:39.069
sort of flailing right
now as a country and

00:40:39.069 --> 00:40:44.150
in trying to get our shit together.

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:46.902
&gt;&gt; While the politics of
it play a massive role and

00:40:46.902 --> 00:40:49.880
would meet all of our lives a lot easier.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:54.920
What can we as individuals perhaps, do on
an individual level in our communities

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:59.884
with the people around us, and then you
shared a little bit about this earlier,

00:40:59.884 --> 00:41:04.034
but tell us more about how you've
had perhaps those uncomfortable

00:41:04.034 --> 00:41:08.980
conversations with your team,
with your family and your community.

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:14.239
&gt;&gt; Well, I think I think number one,
we need to educate ourselves

00:41:14.239 --> 00:41:19.400
the best we can, whether that's,
through our own reading or

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:25.910
just extending ourselves and and
in a way that we haven't done before.

00:41:25.910 --> 00:41:30.060
Bryan Stevenson is another
incredible guest speaker.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:34.905
We had the civil rights lawyer
&gt;&gt; And he talks about proximity,

00:41:34.905 --> 00:41:38.960
and he said, proximity is so important.

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:41.400
And we asked what he mean by proximity?

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:46.580
So well, proximity to the people
that you're trying to help, right?

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:51.714
Puts on the ground,
if you really want to make an impact,

00:41:51.714 --> 00:41:58.050
actually being approximate to
people you're trying to help being,

00:41:58.050 --> 00:42:03.640
in the community in some form
of a grassroots organization.

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:10.693
Movement can, what can you do that
actually is going to not just make

00:42:10.693 --> 00:42:16.910
an impact but make you feel it make
you feel what's actually happening.

00:42:16.910 --> 00:42:21.394
And I think that's what the NBA
has really tried to do,

00:42:21.394 --> 00:42:26.759
every team now is connected with
a local grassroots organization

00:42:26.759 --> 00:42:32.024
that is trying to Improve the lives
of people in the inner city and

00:42:32.024 --> 00:42:36.920
trying to do positive things
in terms of racial relations.

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:41.370
And so
I think that's the challenge is how do we

00:42:41.370 --> 00:42:46.110
actually feel it and be proximate to it.

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:50.110
&gt;&gt; I love that, I had not heard that
analogy with proximity before but

00:42:50.110 --> 00:42:54.630
I think being present and
as close to the source of the difference.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:58.847
Just stepping that Steve,
the George Floyd murder and

00:42:58.847 --> 00:43:04.441
racial injustice is just one issue of
many in justices that are happening

00:43:04.441 --> 00:43:09.409
around the world and some are captured
by the media some are not.

00:43:09.409 --> 00:43:13.599
How do you as a leader and
as a person with a platform,

00:43:13.599 --> 00:43:20.450
decide when to speak up on an issue and
an injustice versus whether to not do so.

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:24.260
And we too will most likely be in
the situations in the business world where

00:43:24.260 --> 00:43:28.639
there's a lot happening out there with
our employees, our staff, our community.

00:43:29.740 --> 00:43:31.100
What's the framework we can use?

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:34.940
How can we think about what do we
need then when do we when do we not?

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:35.750
Now?

00:43:35.750 --> 00:43:36.780
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:42.280
I think what I've realized is to
narrow down my focus to the things

00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:46.990
that I'm really passionate about and
informed about.

00:43:46.990 --> 00:43:52.019
It's really difficult to to go down the
path of hey I want to be an activist and

00:43:52.019 --> 00:43:56.990
start talking about something you
really don't know a whole lot about.

00:43:56.990 --> 00:44:00.855
Those issues are better to set out and
so for me,

00:44:00.855 --> 00:44:06.754
the issues that I'm most interested
in that have really been the biggest

00:44:06.754 --> 00:44:12.580
Dynamics in my own life for
gun violence, and racial equality.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:17.130
I lost my dad to gun
violence when I was 18.

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:18.120
He was 52.

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:25.870
And so gun violence is something that
it affects me deeply and my family.

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:32.179
So I've spent a lot of time with a lot
of the different gun violence groups,

00:44:32.179 --> 00:44:37.590
gun, violence prevention groups,
whether it's Sandy Hook or

00:44:37.590 --> 00:44:42.390
the Brady foundation Giffords,
March for our lives.

00:44:42.390 --> 00:44:45.544
I've done fundraising but
I've also learned a lot and so

00:44:45.544 --> 00:44:47.860
I feel much more comfortable.

00:44:47.860 --> 00:44:50.320
Speaking out about
&gt;&gt; Those things.

00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:55.790
And then the racial justice,
it's just, I'm living every

00:44:55.790 --> 00:45:01.474
day with a group of people whose
lives are directly impacted and

00:45:01.474 --> 00:45:07.803
whose families are directly impacted
by the injustice that exists and

00:45:07.803 --> 00:45:14.980
so It's really easy for me to feel
passionate and connected to those things.

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:15.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's not.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:17.590
It's always going to be a personal choice.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:22.216
But using the idea of being informed and
educated on those topics is

00:45:22.216 --> 00:45:26.770
a droid starting way of desiring
where to lean into those issues?

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:31.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think that's
sort of personal choice too.

00:45:31.350 --> 00:45:36.160
I mean, I've been pretty passionate
about those issues for a long time, but

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:38.460
I just leaned into them.

00:45:38.460 --> 00:45:39.590
Maybe five, six years ago.

00:45:39.590 --> 00:45:42.732
I think I've told this story before,

00:45:42.732 --> 00:45:47.190
but we think it might have
been the 2016 finals.

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:50.693
Maybe it was the conference finals, but

00:45:50.693 --> 00:45:55.810
we had our third moment of
silence in as many weeks.

00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:58.900
This was the Pulse
nightclub massacre Orlando.

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:00.229
&gt;&gt; Yeah, in Orlando.

00:46:00.229 --> 00:46:06.930
&gt;&gt; And we were having this huge game and
we haven't yet another moment of silence.

00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:12.096
&gt;&gt; And it's like so what are we just
going to keep doing this we're just like

00:46:12.096 --> 00:46:17.792
it's great, let's honor the victims and
their families but maybe we should

00:46:17.792 --> 00:46:23.440
excuse my language maybe we should
effing do something about it, right?

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:25.370
&gt;&gt; And it's just, it's infuriating.

00:46:25.370 --> 00:46:26.830
&gt;&gt; It's infuriating and

00:46:26.830 --> 00:46:31.450
I finally just had enough and
I decided just sort of apropos of nothing.

00:46:31.450 --> 00:46:36.333
I was doing a podcast,
local podcasts with Tim Kawakami and

00:46:36.333 --> 00:46:42.094
about basketball and I just I brought
it up and took a lot of body heat for

00:46:42.094 --> 00:46:46.782
it from you know gun rights activists and
learned a lot but

00:46:46.782 --> 00:46:51.468
it was good, it felt good and
it would be in it sort of got,

00:46:51.468 --> 00:46:54.983
I dove in and
I started to get my bearings and

00:46:54.983 --> 00:47:00.140
started to realize where I
could actually make an impact.

00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:01.120
Yeah.

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:04.860
&gt;&gt; Thanks for sharing that I did
not know about that 2016 finals and

00:47:04.860 --> 00:47:07.430
the podcast so that was inspiring.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:11.362
Before we open it up to Q&amp;A wanted to ask
a question which we plan to ask all of our

00:47:11.362 --> 00:47:14.190
speakers this year for the from the top.

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:16.930
Our theme Steve is beyond expectations.

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:23.359
You've certainly in the stories
you've shared right now gone beyond

00:47:23.359 --> 00:47:28.833
the expectations set forth by society for
a NBA player in GM a head coach,

00:47:28.833 --> 00:47:33.175
if you were to perhaps just
summarize what inspires you.

00:47:33.175 --> 00:47:37.580
what motivates you to grow
beyond just those expectations?

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:39.070
What would you say?

00:47:40.810 --> 00:47:48.069
&gt;&gt; I think what I love about coaching and
what inspires me is this feeling of,

00:47:48.069 --> 00:47:52.180
generating joy and happiness as a group.

00:47:53.290 --> 00:47:58.569
Its gives me chills just thinking
about a team that's connected and

00:47:58.569 --> 00:48:01.400
playing well and laughing.

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:04.606
I'm really excited about this year
we added our first exhibition game

00:48:04.606 --> 00:48:05.920
last night.

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:11.783
And you know this Tara, but sometimes you
just feel it and sometimes you don't.

00:48:11.783 --> 00:48:16.430
Like last night, I'm watching our game and
I just had chills,

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:21.876
like this group is connected, and
we're going to have a really fun season.

00:48:21.876 --> 00:48:28.892
And that's what keeps me coming to work
every day and wanting to really succeed,

00:48:28.892 --> 00:48:34.065
is that feeling of momentum
when you just grasp something.

00:48:34.065 --> 00:48:40.579
Then you can feel it and it starts to
grow, and you're just tryna feed it.

00:48:40.579 --> 00:48:46.410
And so many people are interested
in it and enjoying it as well.

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:50.153
And so when our team plays well and
the community gets involved and

00:48:50.153 --> 00:48:55.650
all of a sudden everybody's excited,
that's the best feeling there is for me.

00:48:55.650 --> 00:48:57.833
&gt;&gt; It's great and as a Warrior's fan,

00:48:57.833 --> 00:49:02.201
you're getting chills on the first
preseason games, that is exciting,

00:49:02.201 --> 00:49:06.086
it bodes well for the rest of our
season and I'm excited for that.

00:49:06.086 --> 00:49:10.293
Great let's open it up to questions,
I think we have the first one right

00:49:10.293 --> 00:49:13.670
over there,
you could just introduce yourself please.

00:49:15.490 --> 00:49:19.220
&gt;&gt; Hey Steven, first of all, thank you
very much for being with us tonight.

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:23.930
I'm Sandy Apodaca class of 2023,
I'm managing ODM from Argentina.

00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:28.370
I wanted to draw back in time to 1997,

00:49:28.370 --> 00:49:34.134
game six against the Utah Jazz of
Club Madonna and John Stockton [LAUGH].

00:49:34.134 --> 00:49:38.920
You called a timeout,
25 seconds left on the clock.

00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:40.077
What were you thinking?

00:49:40.077 --> 00:49:44.863
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; How did you manage to convince

00:49:44.863 --> 00:49:48.580
just Michael Jordan?

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:52.828
When you went into the huddle,
you looked him in the eyes and shouted,

00:49:52.828 --> 00:49:55.720
if you need it, if it comes up,
I will be ready.

00:49:56.900 --> 00:49:59.226
&gt;&gt; You might have planted this question.

00:49:59.226 --> 00:50:01.110
&gt;&gt; I planted this question.

00:50:01.110 --> 00:50:05.673
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:05.673 --> 00:50:09.794
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; You just told the story that was

00:50:09.794 --> 00:50:15.082
the moment that every kid
dreams of when you're shooting

00:50:15.082 --> 00:50:19.941
in your driveway or
a you're at school or whatever.

00:50:19.941 --> 00:50:25.900
And you count down the seconds and
NBA championship on the line and

00:50:25.900 --> 00:50:29.700
so to actually live that was incredible.

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:36.086
When you're a kid when you miss,
you just pretend that you got fouled and

00:50:36.086 --> 00:50:40.279
now you go to the free throw line and
you shoot two free throws.

00:50:40.279 --> 00:50:43.480
But when it's happening,
you can't quite have that luxury.

00:50:44.580 --> 00:50:49.604
But it was a moment that I think
it took me a long time to get to

00:50:49.604 --> 00:50:55.360
the point where I could actually
succeed in a situation like that.

00:50:55.360 --> 00:51:00.094
I was very self conscious when
I started playing in the NBA,

00:51:00.094 --> 00:51:02.850
and I didn't really feel clutch.

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:05.260
I felt like I was thinking too much.

00:51:05.260 --> 00:51:09.893
And so I learned a lot, especially
from Michael Jordan who took game

00:51:09.893 --> 00:51:13.995
winning shots almost every week and
missed plenty of them.

00:51:13.995 --> 00:51:14.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:51:14.805 --> 00:51:18.600
didn't seem to bother him that much.

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:23.080
And there was a great lesson in
there that you gotta go for it.

00:51:23.080 --> 00:51:26.230
And you gotta be able to live
with the failure if you don't.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:32.003
But the the best way to succeed is
without considering the failure,

00:51:32.003 --> 00:51:38.020
just playing, just going, and
getting out of your own head.

00:51:38.020 --> 00:51:42.800
And so that was a moment for
me that was probably years in the making.

00:51:42.800 --> 00:51:47.458
And so obviously a great, great moment and
something I'll have forever.

00:51:47.458 --> 00:51:50.240
&gt;&gt; Next question, we have one back there.

00:51:50.240 --> 00:51:55.390
&gt;&gt; Steve, thanks so much for
speaking with us today.

00:51:55.390 --> 00:51:56.260
My name is Tejas.

00:51:56.260 --> 00:51:58.450
I'm a first year MBA student here.

00:51:58.450 --> 00:52:02.250
And my question today is about
building championship culture.

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:06.116
This is tough for me to say as
a lifelong Cleveland Cavaliers fan but

00:52:06.116 --> 00:52:08.332
if you could take us back to 2015.

00:52:08.332 --> 00:52:10.256
What were your top priorities and

00:52:10.256 --> 00:52:13.800
values that you sought to
instil in the organisation?

00:52:13.800 --> 00:52:17.910
And who did you work with and lean on to
help build these values and priorities?

00:52:17.910 --> 00:52:20.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you for the question.

00:52:20.810 --> 00:52:24.427
And congratulations on
the 2016 championship.

00:52:24.427 --> 00:52:28.023
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So when I became a coach, I spent a lot

00:52:28.023 --> 00:52:34.218
of time preparing to coach, but
I didn't become a coach until I was 49.

00:52:34.218 --> 00:52:39.255
So I did some television work
after I was done playing and

00:52:39.255 --> 00:52:44.840
I spent a lot of time preparing and
I talked to a lot of coaches.

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:49.669
And one of the guys who really made
an impact on me was Pete Carroll and

00:52:49.669 --> 00:52:51.806
I went to see Pete in Seattle.

00:52:51.806 --> 00:52:54.030
I love the way his teams played.

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:57.000
And I sat down with him and he just said,

00:52:57.000 --> 00:53:02.680
after we had spent a couple of days
together, and I was watching practice.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:09.090
And he sat down in his office and he said,
so how are you going to coach your team?

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:11.340
I said, you mean like what
offense Are we going to run?

00:53:11.340 --> 00:53:13.879
He said now that stuff
doesn't even matter.

00:53:13.879 --> 00:53:18.429
And I just spent like a year trying to
figure out what our offense was going to

00:53:18.429 --> 00:53:19.184
look like.

00:53:19.184 --> 00:53:21.898
He said really that
stuff you'll figure out.

00:53:21.898 --> 00:53:25.757
He said it's what's it going to
feel like for the players and

00:53:25.757 --> 00:53:31.127
the coaches to come into the gym every day
and what's your culture going to be like.

00:53:31.127 --> 00:53:35.361
And I suppose I had never really
thought of it in those terms, but

00:53:35.361 --> 00:53:40.950
I knew playing for Phil and Pop,
they had built these amazing cultures.

00:53:40.950 --> 00:53:45.979
But Pete really sort of clarified and
he told me the story

00:53:45.979 --> 00:53:51.222
of how he sort of figured out
how to incorporate a culture.

00:53:51.222 --> 00:53:55.810
And he asked me to go home and
think of the four values

00:53:55.810 --> 00:54:01.090
that were the most important
values in my life.

00:54:01.090 --> 00:54:06.164
And that my job as a coach was once
I figured out what those values were,

00:54:06.164 --> 00:54:09.523
was to make those values
come alive every day.

00:54:09.523 --> 00:54:14.752
And that the players would feel that
authenticity since they're my values.

00:54:14.752 --> 00:54:19.182
And so Pete was the guy who
sort of laid it out there and

00:54:19.182 --> 00:54:21.610
it was really fascinating.

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:25.560
Joy and competition were two of them.

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:29.430
Mindfulness was another one.

00:54:29.430 --> 00:54:34.856
Everything that we did was
built on competing and joy and

00:54:34.856 --> 00:54:39.600
mindfulness and
compassion was the fourth one.

00:54:40.800 --> 00:54:42.272
So everything we did and

00:54:42.272 --> 00:54:45.870
the way we treated the team
was based on those values.

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:49.110
We incorporated a ton of joy
into our practice sessions.

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:52.200
We competed at everything,
we kept score of everything.

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:57.804
We treated our players with great
compassion, but making those

00:54:57.804 --> 00:55:04.770
values come alive to me was the lesson of
this is what it means to build a culture.

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:13.583
&gt;&gt; We have time for
one last one over there, we'll wrap up.

00:55:13.583 --> 00:55:18.183
&gt;&gt; Hi, this is Joe Ramirez,
MBA 23 from Mexico.

00:55:18.183 --> 00:55:22.732
So my question is regarding
how you experience

00:55:22.732 --> 00:55:27.690
different joys as a player and as a coach.

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:29.893
I practiced quite quite competitively and

00:55:29.893 --> 00:55:33.624
one of my greatest fears has been
retirement because I feel that is a great

00:55:33.624 --> 00:55:36.570
part of my identity
that I'm going to lose.

00:55:36.570 --> 00:55:38.850
What has been your favorite
part of coaching and

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:43.240
how do you compare that feeling of winning
a championship as a player and as a coach?

00:55:43.240 --> 00:55:44.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah a great question.

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:50.134
I think as player you're very locked in

00:55:50.134 --> 00:55:55.058
on your own game, your own routine.

00:55:55.058 --> 00:55:58.407
And as a coach,
you're thinking about everybody else and

00:55:58.407 --> 00:56:00.768
you're trying to help everyone.

00:56:00.768 --> 00:56:05.633
So actually I've found winning
The championship as a coach was even

00:56:05.633 --> 00:56:10.639
more gratifying than as a player because
you've put in all this energy into

00:56:10.639 --> 00:56:15.970
the whole group and you see how happy
everyone in the organization is.

00:56:15.970 --> 00:56:17.310
And when you're a player,

00:56:17.310 --> 00:56:20.409
it's an amazing feeling too
especially with your teammates.

00:56:20.409 --> 00:56:22.645
But it's more insular.

00:56:22.645 --> 00:56:28.170
It's more you're just locked in on
your own routine day after day.

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:32.340
And coaching is you feel responsible for
everyone.

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:36.580
&gt;&gt; Thanks for answering those, Steve.

00:56:36.580 --> 00:56:38.375
We are almost done.

00:56:38.375 --> 00:56:41.158
Before we let you go,
we want to do a quick lightning round.

00:56:41.158 --> 00:56:42.650
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:56:42.650 --> 00:56:46.763
&gt;&gt; I'll mention a couple of words or
phrases and just,

00:56:46.763 --> 00:56:49.108
you pick one of them, ready?

00:56:49.108 --> 00:56:49.806
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:56:49.806 --> 00:56:52.246
&gt;&gt; Okay, Space Jam 1 or Space Jam 2?

00:56:52.246 --> 00:56:55.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; 1.

00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:58.068
&gt;&gt; 1, okay, so
does that mean Jordan over LeBron?

00:56:58.068 --> 00:57:01.501
Or is that just an indictment
on their acting,

00:57:01.501 --> 00:57:05.490
not their experience, just as actors.

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:07.526
I didn't see Space Jam 2 so.

00:57:07.526 --> 00:57:08.402
&gt;&gt; Okay, there we go.

00:57:08.402 --> 00:57:15.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Messi or Ronaldo?

00:57:15.290 --> 00:57:16.335
&gt;&gt; Messi.

00:57:16.335 --> 00:57:22.609
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; There's my Argentine guy right there.

00:57:22.609 --> 00:57:23.359
&gt;&gt; What's your team?

00:57:23.359 --> 00:57:24.576
&gt;&gt; Liverpool.

00:57:24.576 --> 00:57:25.094
&gt;&gt; Liverpool FC.

00:57:25.094 --> 00:57:29.990
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; We have some Liverpool in here as well.

00:57:29.990 --> 00:57:35.141
Roger Federer, Rafael Nadal,
Novak Djokovic?

00:57:35.141 --> 00:57:35.983
&gt;&gt; Roger Federer.

00:57:35.983 --> 00:57:40.765
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Roger Federer okay, crowd pleaser.

00:57:40.765 --> 00:57:45.987
&gt;&gt; Better dressed team,
1996 Bulls or 2017 Warriors.

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:50.915
&gt;&gt; Do coaches count?

00:57:50.915 --> 00:57:53.667
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You were a player one,

00:57:53.667 --> 00:57:55.054
not a coach on the other.

00:57:55.054 --> 00:58:00.691
&gt;&gt; 2017 Warriors because I was a player
96 Bulls and I brought the level down.

00:58:00.691 --> 00:58:01.350
&gt;&gt; You brought them down.

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:03.738
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's fair.

00:58:03.738 --> 00:58:07.508
[LAUGH] Best city for food in the NBA?

00:58:12.158 --> 00:58:16.116
&gt;&gt; Chicago.

00:58:16.116 --> 00:58:21.300
&gt;&gt; And last one, losing game seven
of the NBA Finals to LeBron James

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:27.295
at home on Father's Day or getting
punched in the face by Michael Jordan?

00:58:27.295 --> 00:58:32.458
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's quite a way to finish

00:58:32.458 --> 00:58:37.913
tonight [LAUGH], really uplifting there.

00:58:37.913 --> 00:58:44.025
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Anyway, I don't know if you know this,

00:58:44.025 --> 00:58:48.134
but I actually went at Michael and

00:58:48.134 --> 00:58:53.284
I actually hit him in
the fist with my jaw.

00:58:53.284 --> 00:58:55.398
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We have an answer, that's an answer.

00:58:55.398 --> 00:59:05.398
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:59:14.764 --> 00:59:16.830
&gt;&gt; I think that was a winner.

00:59:16.830 --> 00:59:17.820
But that was a lot of fun.

00:59:17.820 --> 00:59:20.459
&gt;&gt; I get an ovation for
getting punched, that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:20.459 --> 00:59:23.194
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure I understand, but

00:59:23.194 --> 00:59:24.539
we'll go with it.

00:59:24.539 --> 00:59:27.176
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thanks for your time,

00:59:27.176 --> 00:59:28.540
I think we learned a lot.

00:59:28.540 --> 00:59:29.860
We really appreciate this to you.

00:59:29.860 --> 00:59:35.692
I think you've left us with lessons on
mindfulness, on authenticity, on proximity

00:59:35.692 --> 00:59:40.393
that I think we all can take with us
as we graduate and we walk onto GSB.

00:59:40.393 --> 00:59:45.356
I want to wish you, your family, the team,
best of health and good luck for

00:59:45.356 --> 00:59:47.291
the season and go Warriors.

00:59:47.291 --> 00:59:48.342
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you.

00:59:48.342 --> 00:59:55.469
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:59:55.469 --> 00:59:58.153
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:59:58.153 --> 00:59:59.421
[INAUDIBLE] a glass of water.

00:59:59.421 --> 01:00:04.364
[MUSIC]

